a french appeals court has ruled that  the man accused of funding the rwandan  genocide  can be handed over to a united nations  tribunal  in tanzania felizan kabuga's lawyers had  wanted him to be tried in france where  he was arrested  he faces charges of genocide and crimes  against humanity relating to the horrors  in rwanda during which 800 000 people  were killed in 1994.  here's more about felisan kabuga  the businessman made much of his wealth  from tea farming  felicia and kabuga had strong links to  the former rwandan president juvenal  haberimana  and the then ruling nrmd party dominated  by the hutu  ethnic group kabuki is accused of  setting up  and providing funds for the hutu militia  that carried out  massacres against rival tutsi people and  moderate hutus  he also founded a notorious radio  station that played a key role  in inciting the genocide  a wanted man kabuka evaded arrest for  years  in africa and europe despite reports of  him being cited in different countries  after he was eventually arrested in  paris in may  he appeared before france's supreme  court there  his lawyer said he was in poor health  and in no condition to be tried  elsewhere  and i am now joined by serge brahmetz he  is the chief prosecutor for the  international residual mechanism for  criminal tribunals that is the  international tribunal  handling the rondon genocide trials  welcome to dw  news africa mr branvitz uh feliciano  kabunga  uh wanted to be tried in france your  preference was that he be tried at the  un's tribunal  in tanzania what is the significance of  where the trial takes place  good afternoon in fact it is not the  question of of preference  it's a question about jurisdiction when  the  raunder when the rwanda tribunal closed  a few years ago the residual  competencies were given to the mechanism  especially in relation to the three  remaining main fugitives and kabuka is  one of those three  so based on the statute of the mechanism  which has been put in place by the  security council  today the mechanism is the only  competent jurisdiction to deal with the  kabuka case  okay so you say it is the competent  jurisdiction but mr kabuka has said  through his lawyers  that a u.n court in africa would be  biased  against him is it your view that he is  guaranteed a fair trial  at the u.n tribunal in africa  there's absolutely no doubt about this  you know the  rwanda tribunal and the mechanism is  operating for more than 20 years  there have been dozens and dozens of of  cases our judges are international  judges coming from different parts of  the world  same for the investigators and  prosecutors it is a united  nations tribunal with all the  independence and impartiality  necessary to make sure that it will be a  fair trial  mr brahmos this is a very important case  for the people of rwanda  um and if memory serves me correct  president  paul kagame of rwanda has at some point  hinted that  justice would be better served if mr  booger would be tried in rwanda  in the event that kigali requests to  have mr kabuga tried in rwanda and i  understand that that is a decision that  the u.n security council  would have to make but do you have any  reservations about that  for mr kabuga to be tried in rwanda  no personally i have absolutely no  reservations  in this regard you know in in general  terms as an international prosecutor  i'm always in favor of having  trials taking place in the country where  crimes have been  committed where the victims communities  are living but also where the  perpetrator community is living so where  it is  possible i've always been being  supportive that trials are taking place  at the national level now today this  question of course is not uh on the  agenda if i may say because  um it has been a fugitive  of justice for the rwanda tribunal now  for the mechanism  we have the legal authority to  have this trial taking place at the the  mechanism and we are  absolutely ready to move this trial uh  forward of course if there would be a  decision  by the security council deciding to  change the mandate  as prosecutor well we are updating our  case file and we  follow what uh is decided what is  important  is that a trial is taking place what is  important  is that the victims and survivors will  be hurt  and for me personally um this is the  main  issue not if the trial is taking place  in in arusha or in kigali  okay and are you confident that you're  going to secure a guilty  verdict um recently you were on a  fact-finding mission  in rwanda what can you tell us about  that  well it will be up to to judges to  decide at the end of the trial about  guilt or innocence as prosecutors  of course you know this investigation  started in the 90s  the restaurant is from 97 of course my  predecessors had already collected  evidence  if not no international restaurant or  indictment would be  issued or confirmed now obviously the  case  is has not been active if i may say for  for many years so it's absolutely normal  that as often of the prosecutor  we are updating the existing case file  but we are also looking for additional  evidence to strengthen the the case  uh so i was myself in in kigali  uh only three weeks ago for for two  weeks uh i have in fact  um a permanent team there which would be  there for a number of months it's not  just  effect finding it is really a minimum  for the next six months if not longer  that i will have um several prosecutors  investigators analysts  um in kigali  contacting witnesses which have  testified in the past  updating um information looking for  additional evidence  but it's the the normal way to proceed  if an arrest is taking place many many  years after  the indictment was issued right so the  rest takes place many years after the  indictment issued  what kind of a time span are we looking  at for the actual  trial very briefly if you can  well it's it's up to the judges once a  trial chamber has been  been determined and and put together  now what i can tell you is that based on  on previous cases  with uh with arrests taking place at the  rwanda tribunal yusuf tribunal  most often it takes up to one year  between uh the  the transfer of the the um individual to  the mechanism  and the start of the trial so we will  definitely not see  a trial starting uh somewhere this year  all right that's serge rametz thank you  for that sir  you're almost right
 

